 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the legislative law and the public officers law, in relation to ethics reform; to amend the executive law, in relation to review of disclosure statements; to amend a chapter of the laws of 2009 amending the executive law and other laws relating to governmental ethics and compliance, as proposed in legislative bills numbers S.6064 and A.9032, in relation to the effectiveness thereof; to amend the election law, in relation to a state board of elections enforcement unit, contribution delivery activities by an intermediary, and penalties for violations; and to repeal certain provisions of the election law relating to board of elections enforcement powers",
    "basePrintNo": "S6157",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6157-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§1-c, 1-d, 60, 80 & 81, Leg L; add §74-b, Pub Off L; amd §94, Exec L; amd §22 Chap of 2009 (as proposed in S.6064/A.9032); amd §§3-102, 14-102 & 14-126, rpld & add §3-104, add §16-120, El L",
    "lawSection": "Legislative Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6157\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the legislative law and the public officers law, in\nrelation to ethics reform; to amend the executive law, in relation to\nreview of disclosure statements; to amend a chapter of the laws of\n2009 amending the executive law and other laws relating to\ngovernmental ethics and compliance, as proposed in legislative bills\nnumbers S.6064 and A.9032, in relation to the effectiveness thereof;\nto amend the election law, in relation to a state board of elections\nenforcement unit, contribution delivery activities by an intermediary,\nand penalties for violations; and to repeal certain provisions of the\nelection law relating to board of elections enforcement powers\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nMakes amendments to the ethics reforms proposed in S.6064/A.9032, and\nadds additional language to reform various provisions of the state's\ncampaign finance laws.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 amends paragraphs (i), (ix) and (x) of subdivision (c) of\nsection 1-c of the Legislative Law, to clarify the definition of\n\"lobbying\" to include resolutions, and any other official action by\nthe State Legislature.\n\nSection 2 amends subdivision (b) of section 1-d of the Legislative Law\nto provide that no person who has been a state or local elected\nofficial, a state employee or a registered lobbyist may serve on the\nState Commission on Lobbying and Ethics Compliance for 7 years after\nhaving served in such position.\n\nSection 3 requires the appropriate legislative committees with\njurisdiction over the Joint Legislative Commission on Ethics Standards\nto hold an oversight hearing regarding the commission's annual report\nand recommendations of the commission within 45 days of the release of\nsuch report by the commission.\n\nSection 4 amends paragraph (d)(ii) of subdivision 2 of section 80 of\nthe Legislative Law extends the prohibition on the appointment of\npersons who have been registered lobbyists to the Legislative Office\nof Ethics Investigations from 2 years to 7 years.\n\nSection 5 amends subitem (ii) of item (a) of clause 3 of subparagraph\n(iv) of paragraph (a) of subdivision 4 of section 80 of the\nlegislative law, to alter the list of information that the Legislative\nOffice of Ethics Investigations must submit to the Joint Legislative\nCommission.\n\nSection 6 amends subdivision a of section 81 of the Legislative Law to\nprovide that no person who has been a state or local elected official,\na state employee or a registered lobbyist may serve on the State\nCommission on Lobbying and Ethics Compliance for 7 years after having\nserved in such position.\n\nSection 7 is amended by adding a new Legislative Law § 80(18), which\nrequires the Joint Legislative Commission on Ethics Standards to\ndevelop a protocol for the Legislative Office of Ethics Investigations\nto perform random reviews of annual statements of financial disclosure\nfiled by persons under the Joint Legislative Commission's\njurisdiction.\n\n\nSection 8 adds a new Public Officers Law § 74-b to require state\nofficers to report business dealings with lobbyists.\n\nSection 9 amends subdivision 2 of section 94 of the Executive Law to\nprovide that no person who has been a state or local elected official,\na state employee or a registered lobbyist may serve on the Executive\nEthics and Compliance Commission for 7 years after having served in\nsuch position.\n\nSection 10 is amended by adding a new paragraph o of subdivision 8 of\nsection 94 of the Executive Law, which requires the Executive Ethics\nand Compliance Commission to develop a protocol for the performance of\nrandom reviews of annual statements of financial disclosure filed by\npersons under the Commission's jurisdiction.\n\nSection 11 changes the sunset date of S.6064/A.9032, from 6 years to 3\nyears.\n\nSection 12 makes a conforming change to Election Law § 3-102, to add a\nreference to the State Board of Elections Enforcement Unit, which is\ncreated in section 9.\n\nSection 13 repeals Election Law § 3-104 and adds a new Election Law §\n3-104 to establish the State Board of Elections Enforcement Unit,\nwhich shall have sole authority within the State Board of Elections to\ninvestigate alleged campaign finance complaints and violations.\n\nSection 14 amends Election Law § 14-102 (1) to require greater\ndisclosure of contributor employment and campaign expenditure\ninformation.\n\nSection 15 amends Election Law § 14-126 to increase the maximum civil\npenalty for a violation of New York's campaign finance laws, and to\nmake the willful acceptance of excess contributions subject to a fine\nof two times the amount of the excess plus a fine of up to $10,000,\nand must refund the excess contribution.\n\nSection 16 authorizes the Board of Elections Enforcement Unit to\ninstitute an enforcement in State Supreme Court for violations of the\nArticle 14 of the Elections Law.\n\nSection 17 provides for an effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThis bill seeks to improve the ethics reforms proposed in\nS.6064/A.9032 by:\n\n- requiring random audits of financial disclosure statements filed by\nlegislators and legislative staff in the same manner that the State\nCommission on Lobbying and Ethics Compliance would be directed to\nperform random audits of lobbyists and their clients;\n- requiring state officers to report their business dealings with\nlobbyists;\n- allowing the Legislative Office of Ethics Investigations to initiate\nits own investigations, rather than waiting for a referral of a\ncomplaint;\n- clarifying the definition of \"lobbying\" to include resolutions, and\nany other official action by the State Legislature;\n- extending the prohibition on the appointment of persons who have\nbeen registered lobbyists to the Legislative Office of Ethics\nInvestigations from 2 years to 7 years; and\n- changing the sunset date of S.6064/A.9032, from 6 years to 3 years.\n\n\n\nThe ethics reforms proposed in S.6064/A.9032 will increase the\nindependence and effectiveness of the state's ethics entities and fix\nsome of the weaknesses of the current ethics oversight system.\n\nThe bill also reforms various provisions of the state's campaign\nfinance laws, as follows:\n\n- creates a dedicated enforcement unit within the State Board of\nElections;\n- requires greater disclosure of campaign expenditure information;\n- authorizes the Board of Elections Enforcement Unit to institute an\nenforcement in State Supreme Court for violations of the Article 14 of\nthe Elections Law; and\n- increases penalties for violations of Election Law Article 14.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\n\nThe ethics reforms provisions of this bill (sections 1 through 11)\nwill have no additional fiscal impacts on the state beyond those\nidentified in the sponsor's memorandum for S.6064/A.9032.\n\nThe campaign finance provisions of this bill (sections 12 through 16)\nwill result in increased costs to the state as a result of the\ncreation of a dedicated enforcement unit within the State Board of\nElections.  However, the increase in fines and the Board's ability to\nbring additional enforcement actions should offset at least some of\nthese increased costs.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThe amendments to the Executive Law, the Legislative Law and the\nPublic Officers Law are effective on the same date as provided for in\nS.6064/ A.9032.\nThe amendments to the Election Law are effective 60 days after the\nbill becomes law. However, contributions lawfully received before the\neffective date of this act may not provide the basis for a violation\nof Article 14 of the Election Law.\n",
